<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ekasarana Dharma</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ekasarana <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a> is the Vaishnavite religious movement founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srimanta-sankardev">Srimanta Sankardev</a> in the late fifteenth and sixteenth centuries. It teaches devotion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> as the one God, expressed through congregational prayer, recitation and song rather than sacrifice, and is organised through monasteries called satras and village prayer halls called namghars. It shaped Assamese literature, drama and music and drew many lower-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> and tribal followers.</p></div>
